    <div class="azd-help-content">
        <section data-section="overview">
            <h2>Overview</h2>
            <p>The mission dashboard is a heads-up display over a normal Scene: squad cards on the left, and the
                current objective, mission summary and key intel along the bottom. The map in the middle stays a
                normal, playable map. Panning and zooming move the map, not the dashboard.</p>
            <p>The dashboard only <em>shows</em> information that lives elsewhere, so it is always up to date:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Characters</strong>: their normal Actor and character sheet.</li>
                <li><strong>Mission text</strong>: pages in a mission Journal.</li>
                <li><strong>Which characters and pages belong to the mission</strong>: the Scene.</li>
                <li><strong>Fame and reputation</strong>: one campaign ledger shared by every GM and Scene.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="gm-quick-start">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-gear" inert></i> GM quick start</h2>
            <ol>
                <li>Create or duplicate a Scene and prepare the map, tokens, walls and lights as usual.</li>
                <li>Open the dashboard configuration: right-click the Scene in the Scenes sidebar and choose
                    <strong>Configure Mission Dashboard</strong>. You can also use the Scene configuration's header menu,
                    or the <i class="fa-solid fa-gear" inert></i> button on an existing dashboard.</li>
                <li>Tick <strong>Show mission dashboard</strong>.</li>
                <li>Under <strong>Squad</strong>, pick a character for each slot. The <em>Player Characters</em> folder
                    is listed first. Add, remove and reorder slots as needed; empty slots are hidden from players.</li>
                <li>Under <strong>Mission Journal</strong>, click <strong>Create mission Journal</strong>. This creates
                    Summary, Current Objective, Key Intel and GM Notes pages and selects them. You can also choose an
                    existing Journal.</li>
                <li>Write the mission in the Journal. It starts hidden from players; click <strong>Share with
                    players</strong> when you're ready. GM Notes always stays GM-only.</li>
                <li>Optionally add <strong>People of Note</strong>, then click <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
                <li>During play, use <i class="fa-solid fa-plus" inert></i> on a panel when the objective changes, and
                    the <i class="fa-solid fa-ranking-star" inert></i> button to record Fame and reputation.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="azd-help-note">Duplicating a Scene keeps its dashboard, but the copy still uses the <strong>same</strong>
                mission Journal pages. For a new mission, create or choose a different Journal in the copy's configuration.</p>
        </section>

        <section data-section="player-quick-start">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-users" inert></i> Player quick start</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Your character's card updates by itself when wounds, strain, gear or critical injuries change on
                    the sheet.</li>
                <li>Click <i class="fa-solid fa-pen" inert></i> next to <strong>Desire</strong> on your card to write
                    what you'd like your character to pursue or experience. The GM reads these for plot hooks.</li>
                <li>Click a name or portrait to open the sheet, and a critical injury to open its details.</li>
                <li>Use ‹ and › on the mission panels to read earlier objectives and summaries.</li>
                <li>Need the whole map? Click <i class="fa-solid fa-eye-slash" inert></i>. It only hides the dashboard
                    on your screen, and the <strong>Dashboard</strong> button at the top brings it back.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="cards">
            <h2>Squad cards</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Wounds</strong> and <strong>Strain</strong> show the current value against the threshold,
                    as on the sheet. A value above the threshold is red and marked <em>over</em>. The bar stops at full,
                    but the number always shows the real value.</li>
                <li><strong>Soak</strong>, <strong>Def M</strong> (melee) and <strong>Def R</strong> (ranged) are the
                    sheet values, including gear and effects. A dash (—) means the value isn't set on the sheet.</li>
                <li><strong>Critical injuries</strong> are listed with their severity. Click one to open it.</li>
                <li>Cards always show the character's own Actor. Player-character tokens are normally linked, so damage
                    on them shows up. Damage on an <em>unlinked</em> token (a copy) doesn't.</li>
                <li>On small screens the cards become compact. Use <i class="fa-solid fa-expand" inert></i> on a card to
                    see everything.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="desire">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-pen" inert></i> Desire &amp; <i class="fa-solid fa-image" inert></i> art</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Desire</strong> belongs to the character and carries over to every mission. The character's
                    owner and GMs can edit it, up to 500 characters. <strong>Save</strong> or Ctrl+Enter saves;
                    <strong>Cancel</strong> or Escape discards your changes.</li>
                <li>If someone else changes the Desire while you're typing, your text is kept. A warning shows their
                    version, and you can switch to it or save yours.</li>
                <li><strong>Full-body art</strong>: hover over the card art and click <i class="fa-solid fa-image" inert></i>.
                    Pick an image, or paste a path from your GM. The art is shown whole, not cropped. Leave the path
                    empty to go back to the portrait. If an image fails to load, the card falls back to the portrait.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="mission">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-crosshairs" inert></i> Mission panels</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Current objective</strong> (orange) answers “what do we do next?” Keep it short. It stays
                    visible even when the panels are collapsed.</li>
                <li><strong>Mission summary</strong> answers “why are we here?”</li>
                <li><strong>Key intel</strong> holds people, places and clues, with People of Note cards at the top.</li>
                <li>Everything is normal Journal content: links to Actors and Journals, images and formatting all work.
                    Secret blocks are only shown to GMs.</li>
                <li>GMs: <i class="fa-solid fa-pen-to-square" inert></i> opens the page's editor. Changes appear for
                    everyone as soon as you save.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="history">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-clock-rotate-left" inert></i> Entry history</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Each mission panel keeps a history of pages, and the newest one is shown. With more than one entry,
                    ‹ 2/3 › arrows appear. Browsing only changes your own screen; <strong>Back to current</strong>
                    returns to the newest.</li>
                <li>GMs: <i class="fa-solid fa-plus" inert></i> creates the next page (for example <em>Current Objective 2</em>),
                    makes it current for everyone and opens it for editing. The previous page stays as history,
                    unchanged.</li>
                <li>GMs can also add, reorder or remove history entries in the configuration. Removing an entry doesn't
                    delete the page.</li>
                <li>Players only see entries they can read. If the newest page is hidden from them, they keep seeing the
                    previous one.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="people">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-address-card" inert></i> People of Note</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>GMs add them in the configuration: choose the NPC's Actor, then fill in role, status, relationship and
                    a note. The name and portrait come from the Actor.</li>
                <li>A player only sees a person whose Actor they can see. Set the NPC's ownership to <strong>Limited</strong>
                    or higher (right-click the Actor, then Configure Ownership). Limited shows the name and portrait but
                    not the sheet. People players can't see are marked <strong>GM only</strong>.</li>
                <li>Everything typed into these fields is visible to players. Keep secrets in GM Notes.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="reputation">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-ranking-star" inert></i> Fame &amp; faction reputation</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Fame</strong> is how well known the squad is. <strong>Faction reputation</strong> is how much
                    a particular organization likes them. Both are plain whole numbers that start at 0. There are no
                    caps and no automatic effects on rolls.</li>
                <li>Open the campaign window with the <strong>Fame</strong> button in the dashboard header. Everyone can
                    see the totals and the history.</li>
                <li>GMs: the first time, click <strong>Create ledger</strong>. Then add factions and use +1 / −1. Every
                    change needs a <strong>reason</strong>, which players can read. For an existing campaign, enter the
                    current values as opening balances, e.g. “+3: Opening balance”.</li>
                <li>Fix mistakes with <i class="fa-solid fa-rotate-left" inert></i> <strong>Correct</strong>. It adds a
                    reversing entry and marks the original as corrected. Archiving a faction hides it but keeps its
                    history.</li>
                <li>The ledger is shared by every GM and Scene, and two GMs adjusting at the same moment both get
                    counted.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="screen">
            <h2>Your screen</h2>
            <p>These only change <strong>your</strong> screen, never anyone else's:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><i class="fa-solid fa-eye-slash" inert></i> <strong>Focus map</strong>: hides the dashboard. The
                    <strong>Dashboard</strong> button at the top of the screen brings it back. You can also bind a key
                    to <em>Toggle mission dashboard</em> in Configure Controls.</li>
                <li><i class="fa-solid fa-table-list" inert></i> <strong>Compact cards</strong>: cycles between Auto,
                    On and Off. Auto turns compact mode on for small screens.</li>
                <li><i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down" inert></i> <strong>Collapse panels</strong>: shrinks the mission
                    panels to one bar that still shows the objective.</li>
                <li>Scrolling over the dashboard scrolls the dashboard; scrolling over the map zooms the map.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-section="permissions">
            <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lock" inert></i> Who sees what</h2>
            <table class="azd-help-table">
                <thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Who</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Configure the dashboard, add history entries</td><td>GMs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>See a card's stats and Desire</td><td>Observer or higher on that character</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>See only name and art</td><td>Limited on that character</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Edit Desire or art</td><td>The character's owners and GMs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Read a mission panel</td><td>Anyone who can read that Journal page</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Record Fame or reputation</td><td>GMs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Hide, compact or collapse</td><td>Everyone, for themselves</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section data-section="tips">
            <h2>Tips &amp; troubleshooting</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>A panel says “Classified” or “No briefing available”</strong>: the page isn't shared with
                    players yet. Use <em>Share with players</em> in the configuration, or change the Journal's ownership.</li>
                <li><strong>A character card is missing for a player</strong>: empty slots are hidden. A card for a
                    deleted Actor shows as unavailable.</li>
                <li><strong>A Person of Note is missing for players</strong>: set that NPC's Actor to Limited.</li>
                <li><strong>The squad column is short</strong>: collapse the Scene navigation, or turn on compact cards.</li>
                <li><strong>The dashboard is gone</strong>: check for the <strong>Dashboard</strong> button at the top of
                    the screen (it may be hidden on your screen), or ask a GM whether this Scene has the dashboard
                    enabled.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
    </div>
</div>
